rd\": \"deliberate\",\n  1385→      \"flag_reason\": \"homograph\",\n  1386→      \"occurrences\": 1,\n  1387→      \"first_position\": 11611,\n  1388→      \"chapter_indices\": [\n  1389→        1\n  1390→      ],\n  1391→      \"context_examples\": [\n  1392→        \"...his group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n  1393→      ],\n  1394→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  1395→      \"ipa\": null,\n  1396→      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n  1397→      \"audio_url\": null,\n  1398→      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n  1399→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n  1400→    }\n  1401→  ],\n  1402→  \"glossary\": null,\n  1403→  \"overview\": {\n  1404→    \"structure\": {\n  1405→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1406→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1407→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1408→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1409→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1410→    },\n  1411→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1412→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the grip of a merciless plague known as the Red Death, a disease marked by excruciating pain, violent bleeding, and the telltale scarlet stains that signal death. As the world outside crumbles into despair, Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and defiant spirit, retreats with a thousand courtiers into the isolated, fortress-like abbey he has transformed into a sanctuary of pleasure. Sealing off all entrances and indulging in endless revelry, he attempts to escape the grim reality beyond, hosting a lavish masked ball in seven opulently decorated chambers, each with stained-glass windows that reflect their unique hues. The final chamber, however, is a chilling black room with blood-red panes and a massive ebony clock that chimes each hour, bringing silence and unease to the festivities—reminders of time’s inexorable march and the dread lurking just beyond the revelry.\\n\\nAs the clock strikes midnight, a mysterious figure appears, dressed as the Red Death itself—clad in blood-stained garments and wearing a mask that resembles a corpse. The figure moves through the seven chambers with eerie calm, unchallenged by the terrified guests who, despite their fear, remain paralyzed by dread. Prince Prospero, enraged and humiliated by the intrusion, draws a dagger and pursues the figure into the black chamber, only to collapse dead upon confronting it. The revelers, now realizing the truth, discover the figure is hollow—an empty shell—revealing that the Red Death has infiltrated their sanctuary not as a visitor, but as an inevitable force. One by one, the courtiers fall, succumbing to the plague as the clock stops and darkness consumes the abbey, ending their futile attempt to escape fate.\\n\\nThe narrative unfolds as a chilling allegory of human arrogance and the futility of denying mortality. Prince Prospero’s isolation and indulgence represent a dangerous illusion of control, while the masked figure symbolizes the inescapable nature of death. The turning point occurs when the Red Death enters the ball not as a threat from outside, but as a manifestation of the very fear the revelers tried to suppress. The climax, in the black chamber, is both physical and symbolic—the prince’s final confrontation with his own mortality, and the collapse of his illusion of safety. The resolution is absolute: no one survives, and the abbey, once a bastion of life, becomes a tomb, underscoring the story’s central truth—that death is the great equalizer, indifferent to wealth, power, or denial.\",\n  1413→      \"themes\": [\n  1414→        \"identity\",\n  1415→        \"ambition\",\n  1416→        \"loss\"\n  1417→      ],\n  1418→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1419→    },\